Upstream-Status: Pending
Exit: Drop when a btrfs-progs release removes empty block groups in `mkfs.btrfs --shrink`.
Subject: btrfs-progs: mkfs: remove empty block groups before shrinking

While --rootdir fills the filesystem, a new chunk is allocated whenever the
existing ones of that type are 70% used (btrfs_try_chunk_alloc), so a
populated filesystem ends with a run of block groups that hold nothing.
--shrink only cuts the device back to the end of the last device extent, so
those empty block groups stay in the image: about 2.9 GiB of a 7.7 GiB image
of the Dakota live ISO's seed filesystem.

Remove empty data and metadata block groups before the device size is
computed. An empty block group goes only when a used one of the same type
and profile stays, empty metadata block groups stay unless the used ones
have 32 MiB free, and system, mixed and remap block groups are left alone.

The shrink also moves to the end of mkfs, after the UUID tree rebuild, the
temporary chunk cleanup and the quota setup, because each of those needs
free metadata space and the removed block groups were where it came from.

Known limits, which is why this is a local patch and not an upstream
submission as written:

- The saving depends on the chunk layout. An empty block group in front of
  a used one becomes a hole inside the image and saves nothing, because the
  shrink still only trims the tail. files/live-iso/check-seed.py fails the
  ISO build when that happens.
- With -O squota the image comes out larger than unpatched: quota setup now
  runs before the shrink and allocates a metadata block group the reserve
  rule then keeps. The ISO build does not use quotas.
- The better fix is at the cause, the allocation ahead of need in --rootdir.

Only the live ISO build uses this copy of mkfs.btrfs. It is not in the image.

